use  damage and then dysfunction  to this layer of endothelial cells  now when this damage happened  these cells where the damage has  occurred can also start releasing free  radicals basically like reactive oxygen  species  remember that reactive oxygen species  includes free radicals and also some  other types of things like you know  peroxides and so on  so the reactive oxygen species get  produced  because of that damage or following that  damage  then  this damage means that the arterial  walls become more permeable to ldl  remember that's low density lipoprotein  this is a specific way in which  cholesterol  is moved through the body and so we've  got our ldl particles i've drawn them  here in green  going through the bloodstream  and so this damage  makes the arterial walls more permeable  to ldl allowing the ldl  to actually go  you know across the endothelium into the  tissue on the other side of the  endothelial layer and this tissue is  called the tunica entoma so you get ldl  increasing  in concentration because of the damage  to the endothelial  cells then you've got adhesions on the  damaged endothelial cells capture  monocytes  so i could i'm going to kind of draw  basically some like a receptor right  there you've got an adhesion protein  that will capture a circulating monocyte  remember that a monocyte is a type of  white blood cell it's part of the immune  system so the receptor the adhesion and  underlying that right there  adhesions on the damaged endothelial  cells will capture the monocytes  and this will  uh increase  the  their sort of movement their migration  into the tunica  entoma  now when they enter when the monocytes  enter the tissue the tunica intima  they are then going to develop in  something called macrophages so  monocytes are the ones circulating in  the blood and then when they enter a  specific tissue  they are stimulated to develop into  macrophages so we've got the monocyte  moves in and now it's a macrophage  and they are also going to be producing  free radicals so some of these reactive  oxygen species so they were already we  already had reactive oxygen species free  radicals being produced by the damaged  endothelial cells and now they're being  produced by these macrophages as well  now these free radicals that are being  produced they will  oxidize  ldl  so right here  when the ldl is oxidized  by these free radicals that are coming  either from the damaged endothelial  cells  or from the macrophages  i started drawing the oxidized ldl in  red so the concentration of oxidized ldl  is also increasing in the tunica entity  and this has the unfortunate side  effects of attracting even more  monocytes  um basically you've got even more  monocytes that because of the increase  in oxidized ldl here you've got even  more monocytes that are going to come  right down here let me grab my purple  marker  and i'll draw another of these  uh  macrophages  so this is a kind of  basically positive feedback loop  um we might also call it a vicious cycle  right this is not the kind of positive  feedback loop you want to happen  remember that positive feedback loop  just means that the signal basically  keeps moving further and further away  from homeostasis so there's these these  signals and you have more and more and  more  activity and problems cropping up  i also want to mention just that the  movement of the monocytes into the  tissue i'm going to put this in  parentheses here  is through  a  a process called diapedesis that's just  the process through which they kind of  flatten  and move through the small space to get  into the tissues  the signaling  that encourages this behavior is the  chemokine signaling so basically the  more you have  oxidized ldl down here the more you have  macrophages  secreting chemokines the more you have  more monocytes that are going to be  coming in and turning into macrophages  and secreting more chemokines  to attract more to attract more  monocytes and s